=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The network is healthy. Exhaustive testing from every angle confirms there
is no fault introduced by the reported maintenance.

Switch4 (the "Campus 2 access switch") carries both VLAN5 and VLAN6 on its
uplink trunk to sshswitch: eth0.5 is in br_vlan5 and eth0.6 is in br_vlan6,
both UP and forwarding. On the sshswitch side, the corresponding sub-interfaces
(eth0.5→br_v5, eth0.6→br_v6) are also UP, and sshswitch holds the correct L3
gateways — br_v5 at 10.5.0.254/24 and br_v6 at 10.6.0.254/24. Sshswitch runs
OSPF and explicitly advertises both prefixes (network 10.5.0.0/24 area 0,
network 10.6.0.0/24 area 0), so every downstream router — including router17,
router16, and mswitch0 — carries valid return routes to both subnets.

End-to-end reachability is fully verified: pc1 (10.5.0.1) and pc3 (10.6.0.3)
both reach their respective gateways, inter-VLAN targets, mswitch0 (10.2.2.2),
all VLAN 10/20/30 gateway addresses, and the WAN segment (192.168.10.1) — all
with zero packet loss. The reverse direction is equally healthy: pc5 (VLAN10,
172.16.10.5) pings both pc1 and pc3 without any drops.

The ticket's premise is false. No VLAN fell off the trunk, no interface is
down, no route is missing, and there is no impact on user connectivity.
Root cause: the reported outage does not exist; the network has been
functioning correctly throughout.

Corrective action: no change required. The helpdesk ticket should be closed
as a false alarm — the maintenance visit left the switching and routing
configuration intact and fully operational.
=== END ===
